// PROBLEM BANK: WEEK 19 - MULTI-THREADED APPLICATIONS
// 100 Problems spanning actor foundations, scaling, synchronization, and deadlock prevention

===== CATEGORY 19.1: ACTOR FOUNDATIONS (30 Problems) =====

// SECTION 19.1.1-19.1.10: Basic Actor Patterns

[19.1.1]
Problem: Simple Work Distributor Actor
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Create a work distribution actor that:
  - Accepts work requests (strings)
  - Processes each work item immediately
  - Tracks total completion count
  - Returns completion count on request
TestCases:
  - Create actor, send 5 work items, verify count = 5
  - Send 10 items, check count increases correctly
  - Start at 0, increment by 1 each time
Tags: actor-basics, state-management

[19.1.2]
Problem: Echo Actor with Message Logging
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Implement an echo actor that:
  - Receives any message
  - Logs message to internal vector
  - Returns message back to sender
  - Counts total messages received
TestCases:
  - Send "hello" -> receive "ECHO: hello"
  - Send 100 messages -> verify count = 100
  - Retrieve logs -> check order preserved
Tags: actor-basics, message-handling, logging

[19.1.3]
Problem: Task Counter Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Create task counter that responds to:
  - CompleteTask: increment completed count
  - FailTask: increment failed count
  - GetStatus: return (completed, failed, pending)
TestCases:
  - Complete 5, fail 2 -> GetStatus = (5, 2, 7)
  - Pending = total_submitted - (completed + failed)
  - Handle 0 tasks -> (0, 0, 0)
Tags: state-machine, counters

[19.1.4]
Problem: Accumulator Actor
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Implement accumulator that:
  - Receives numeric values
  - Sums all received values
  - Reset command clears sum
  - Returns running total on request
TestCases:
  - Add 10, 20, 30 -> GetSum = 60
  - Reset -> GetSum = 0
  - Add negative: -5 -> GetSum = -5
Tags: arithmetic, state-tracking

[19.1.5]
Problem: Request-Response Pattern
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Implement request-response pattern:
  - Actor accepts (request_id, payload) pairs
  - Processes asynchronously
  - Can handle multiple outstanding requests
  - Returns (request_id, response) tuples
TestCases:
  - Send request 1, 2, 3
  - Get back responses in any order (not necessarily FIFO)
  - Verify IDs match
Tags: correlation, request-response

[19.1.6]
Problem: State Machine Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Create FSM actor with states: Idle, Processing, Complete
  Valid transitions:
  - Idle -> Processing (on work request)
  - Processing -> Complete (on finish)
  - Complete -> Idle (on reset)
  Reject invalid transitions
TestCases:
  - Idle + work -> Processing
  - Processing + finish -> Complete
  - Processing + finish again -> rejected
  - Complete + reset -> Idle
Tags: state-machine, validation

[19.1.7]
Problem: Rate-Limited Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Create rate limiter that:
  - Allows max 10 messages/second
  - Queues excess messages
  - Processes queued messages in order
  - Returns queue length on status check
TestCases:
  - Send 25 messages fast -> queue 15
  - Wait 1s -> queue decrements by ~10
  - Send 5 more -> queue has ~10
Tags: rate-limiting, queuing

[19.1.8]
Problem: Result Collector Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Collector waits for N responses from workers:
  - Collects up to 5 responses
  - Has timeout (3s)
  - Returns all collected responses
  - Errors if timeout before collecting N
TestCases:
  - Collect 3 responses -> return vector of 3
  - Timeout after 1s with 2 responses -> error
  - Collect under timeout -> success
Tags: timeout, collection

[19.1.9]
Problem: Retry-Dispatch Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Dispatcher sends to primary, retries on backup:
  - Send to primary worker
  - If fails, send to backup
  - Track success and failure
  - Return final result
TestCases:
  - Primary succeeds -> count success
  - Primary fails, backup succeeds -> count 1 retry
  - Both fail -> count failed
Tags: fault-tolerance, retry-logic

[19.1.10]
Problem: Circuit Breaker Actor
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Implement circuit breaker:
  - Closed: requests pass through
  - Open (> 5 failures): reject requests
  - Half-open: test single request
  - On success: return to Closed
  - Track failure count
TestCases:
  - 5 failures -> state = Open
  - In Open -> reject incoming requests
  - Test request succeeds -> state = Closed
Tags: resilience, circuit-breaker

// SECTION 19.1.11-19.1.20: Message Passing

[19.1.11]
Problem: Typed Message Enum
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Create enum with message variants:
  - ProcessMessage(String)
  - ResponseMessage(String)
  - EventMessage(String)
  - StatusMessage
  Serialize/deserialize for logging
TestCases:
  - Create each variant
  - Convert to string representation
  - Parse back (if implementing parser)
Tags: enums, message-types

[19.1.12]
Problem: Message Router
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Router that:
  - Maps target name to handler
  - Routes message to correct handler
  - Returns error if unknown target
  - Logs routing decisions
TestCases:
  - Route to "worker1" -> delivered
  - Route to "unknown" -> error
  - Multi-target routing -> deliver to all
Tags: routing, message-handling

[19.1.13]
Problem: Request-Response Correlator
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Assign ID to each request:
  - new_request() -> assign unique ID, store mapping
  - handle_response(id, response) -> return matching request
  - Timeout unmatched requests (> 1s)
  - Detect duplicate IDs
TestCases:
  - Submit request -> get ID
  - Receive response with ID -> match
  - Timeout after 1s of waiting
Tags: correlation, request-response

[19.1.14]
Problem: Fire-and-Forget Dispatcher
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Send message with no response expected:
  - Send message
  - Don't wait/block
  - Track delivery (best effort)
  - Count sent messages
TestCases:
  - Send 100 -> count = 100
  - Send no response needed -> success
  - Handle backpressure gracefully
Tags: async, fire-and-forget

[19.1.15]
Problem: Ordered Delivery Verifier
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Verify messages arrive in order:
  - Send messages with sequence numbers
  - Detect out-of-order reception
  - Return ordering violation count
TestCases:
  - Send 1,2,3,4,5 -> received in order
  - Receive 1,3,2,4,5 -> detect disorder
  - Count violations (2 out of 5)
Tags: ordering, verification

[19.1.16]
Problem: Message Deduplicator
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Remove duplicate messages:
  - Track message IDs seen
  - Discard duplicates
  - Maintain ordering
  - Return duplicate count
TestCases:
  - Send A,B,A,C,B,A -> process A,B,C (3 duplicates)
  - Check order: A,B,C (not scrambled)
Tags: deduplication, state-tracking

[19.1.17]
Problem: Priority Message Queue
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Queue with priorities:
  - High priority processed first
  - Medium, then Low
  - Prevent starvation (low eventually processed)
  - FIFO within same priority
TestCases:
  - High=1, Med=2, Low=3 -> process High first
  - After 3 Highs, process 1 Medium (prevent starvation)
Tags: priority-queue, fairness

[19.1.18]
Problem: Message Batching Actor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Batch messages for efficiency:
  - Collect N messages (N=5)
  - Process as single batch
  - Reduce per-message overhead
  - Handle partial batches on timeout
TestCases:
  - Send 12 messages -> 2 batches of 5 + 1 partial
  - Batch 1 has 5 items -> process together
Tags: batching, efficiency

[19.1.19]
Problem: Message Compression
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Compress for large messages:
  - Compress on send (simple deflate)
  - Decompress on receive
  - Track compression ratio
  - Measure overhead
TestCases:
  - Large message (1000 bytes) -> compress
  - Decompress -> verify original
  - Ratio = compressed/original (usually 50-80%)
Tags: compression, optimization

[19.1.20]
Problem: Message Versioning
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Support multiple message formats:
  - v1 format: {type, data}
  - v2 format: {type, data, timestamp}
  - Auto-migrate v1 -> v2
  - Track version used
TestCases:
  - Receive v1 -> migrate to v2 internally
  - Receive v2 -> use directly
  - All stored responses in v2
Tags: versioning, migration

// SECTION 19.1.21-19.1.30: Lifecycle & Supervision

[19.1.21]
Problem: Actor Lifecycle Tracker
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Track actor states:
  - Created -> Running -> Stopped
  - Record timestamps
  - Count state changes
  - Query current state
TestCases:
  - Create actor -> state = Created
  - Start -> state = Running (t1 recorded)
  - Stop -> state = Stopped (t2 recorded)
  - Total changes = 2
Tags: lifecycle, state-machine

[19.1.22]
Problem: Graceful Shutdown Handler
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  On shutdown:
  - Drain pending messages
  - Flush state to storage
  - Report final statistics
  - Prevent new messages
TestCases:
  - 10 pending messages -> drain all
  - Shutdown reports count
  - New messages rejected after shutdown
Tags: shutdown, draining

[19.1.23]
Problem: Actor Crash Recovery
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  On crash (error processing message):
  - Detect failure
  - Restart from initial state
  - Preserve some history (last 10 messages)
  - Alert supervisor
TestCases:
  - Process 100 messages, crash on 50
  - Restart, recover history
  - Continue processing
Tags: recovery, fault-tolerance

[19.1.24]
Problem: Restart Strategy Evaluator
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Exponential backoff for restarts:
  - 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s
  - Max 5 restarts in 60s window
  - Circuit break after threshold
  - Track restart history
TestCases:
  - Restart attempt 1 -> wait 1s
  - Restart attempt 3 -> wait 4s
  - Attempt 6 -> circuit break (exceed max)
Tags: exponential-backoff, circuit-breaker

[19.1.25]
Problem: Supervision Tree
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Parent supervises N children:
  - Add/remove children
  - One child crashes -> restart
  - Multiple crashes -> escalate to parent
  - Track tree structure
TestCases:
  - Add 3 children -> can list all
  - Child 1 crashes -> parent detects, restarts
  - Child crashes repeatedly -> escalate
Tags: supervision, hierarchical

[19.1.26]
Problem: Health Check System
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Ping actor, expect pong:
  - Send ping every 1s
  - Expect pong within 500ms
  - Timeout = unhealthy
  - Mark failed, trigger restart
TestCases:
  - Healthy actor -> always pongs
  - Broken actor -> no pong -> restart
Tags: health-checks, monitoring

[19.1.27]
Problem: Crash Reporter
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Log crashes comprehensively:
  - Timestamp of crash
  - Last message received
  - Error reason
  - Stack (if available)
  - Actor name
TestCases:
  - Crash actor -> log includes all info
  - Retrieve crash log -> verify complete
Tags: logging, debugging

[19.1.28]
Problem: Escalation Policy
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Escalate failures up tree:
  - Child crash -> parent restart
  - Parent crash -> stop all children
  - Top-level crash -> system shutdown
  - Implement strategy
TestCases:
  - Child crashes -> parent recovers
  - Parent crashes -> children stopped
  - Root crashes -> shutdown
Tags: escalation, supervision

[19.1.29]
Problem: Recovery Metrics
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Track recovery success:
  - Crash count per actor
  - Time to recovery (crash -> healthy)
  - Success rate after recovery
  - Average recovery time
TestCases:
  - Actor crashes, recovers in 2s -> record
  - Track 10 crashes -> calculate average
Tags: metrics, observability

[19.1.30]
Problem: Actor Census
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Enumerate all running actors:
  - List actor names
  - Show state (running, failed, idle)
  - Count by type
  - Memory per actor
TestCases:
  - 50 workers running -> list all
  - 5 failed -> show separately
  - Total memory usage
Tags: introspection, monitoring

===== CATEGORY 19.2: SCALING (30 Problems) =====

// SECTION 19.2.1-19.2.15: Pools & Load Distribution

[19.2.1]
Problem: Fixed-Size Actor Pool
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Create pool with 10 workers:
  - Pre-allocate all actors
  - Distribute work round-robin
  - Track completion
  - Handle overflow gracefully
TestCases:
  - 10 workers, 100 work items
  - All items processed
  - No item lost
Tags: pooling, fixed-size

[19.2.2]
Problem: Dynamic Pool Sizing
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Auto-size pool based on load:
  - Start with 5 workers
  - Add worker if queue depth > 100
  - Remove if idle > 10s
  - Min 2, max 50
TestCases:
  - Queue fills -> add workers
  - Queue empties -> remove workers
  - Stay within bounds
Tags: dynamic-sizing, auto-scaling

[19.2.3]
Problem: Work Distribution Scheduler
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Distribute to least-busy worker:
  - Track queue depth per worker
  - Always assign to smallest queue
  - Prevent starvation
  - Measure variance
TestCases:
  - 3 workers with queues [1, 5, 2]
  - New work -> assign to worker 0 (queue now [2, 5, 2])
  - Variance = std_dev of queue depths
Tags: load-balancing, least-busy

[19.2.4]
Problem: Worker Pool with Backpressure
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Bounded queue (capacity 1000):
  - Reject new work if full
  - Return error to sender
  - Sender can buffer/retry
  - Measure rejection rate
TestCases:
  - Enqueue 1000 items -> all accepted
  - Enqueue 1001st -> rejected
  - Rejection % = (1 / 1001) * 100%
Tags: backpressure, bounded-queue

[19.2.5]
Problem: Round-Robin Distributor
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Cycle through workers:
  - Worker 1, 2, 3, ..., N, 1, 2...
  - Simple, no state tracking needed
  - Measure load variance
TestCases:
  - 50 items, 10 workers
  - Load should be ~5 per worker
  - Variance low
Tags: round-robin, simple

[19.2.6]
Problem: Hash-Based Distributor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Assign by key hash:
  - hash(key) % num_workers -> worker ID
  - Same key always same worker
  - Maintains ordering per key
  - Handle hash collisions
TestCases:
  - Items with key "A" -> always worker X
  - Items with key "B" -> always worker Y
  - Load distribution follows key distribution
Tags: hash-based, affinity

[19.2.7]
Problem: Work Batching Pool
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Collect items into batches:
  - Batch size = 5
  - Send batch to worker
  - Reduce per-item overhead
  - Handle partial batches at end
TestCases:
  - 12 items -> 2 batches of 5 + 1 of 2
  - Batch 1 processed together
  - All items completed
Tags: batching, throughput

[19.2.8]
Problem: Priority Work Queue
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Process high-priority first:
  - High > Medium > Low priorities
  - FIFO within same priority
  - Prevent Low from starving (eventually process)
TestCases:
  - Queue: [H1, L1, M1, H2]
  - Order: H1, H2, M1, L1
Tags: priority-queue, fairness

[19.2.9]
Problem: Work Stealing Pool
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Idle workers steal from busy:
  - Each worker has own queue
  - If own queue empty, steal from neighbor
  - Reduces idle time
  - Improves load balance
TestCases:
  - Worker A queue full, B empty -> B steals from A
  - Measure throughput improvement (vs. simple pool)
Tags: work-stealing, load-balance

[19.2.10]
Problem: Work Timeouts
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Work items have deadline:
  - Each item has 5s timeout
  - Cancel if not started by deadline
  - Track cancelled count
  - Restart timed-out workers
TestCases:
  - Submit work, wait 6s -> cancelled if not started
  - Measure cancellation rate
Tags: timeout, deadline

[19.2.11]
Problem: Result Collection
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Collect results from worker:
  - Results sent back to submitter
  - Maintain order (optional)
  - Handle failures
  - Timeout if no result
TestCases:
  - Submit 100 items -> collect 100 results
  - All results received
  - No missing results
Tags: result-handling, collection

[19.2.12]
Problem: Pool Statistics
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Measure pool performance:
  - Throughput (items/sec)
  - Latency (min, max, avg)
  - Queue depth over time
  - Worker utilization
TestCases:
  - Process 1000 items in 1s
  - Throughput = 1000 items/sec
  - Report p50, p95, p99 latencies
Tags: metrics, observability

[19.2.13]
Problem: Pool Drain Functionality
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Graceful shutdown:
  - Stop accepting new work
  - Process all pending work
  - Then terminate workers
TestCases:
  - 100 items in queue
  - Call drain()
  - All 100 process
  - No worker accepts new work
Tags: shutdown, graceful

[19.2.14]
Problem: Worker Recycling
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Restart worker after N items:
  - Process 100 items
  - Restart worker (reset state)
  - Continue processing
  - Prevent memory leaks
TestCases:
  - 1000 items, restart every 100
  - Total restarts = 10
  - All items processed correctly
Tags: lifecycle, memory-management

[19.2.15]
Problem: Adaptive Pool
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Adapt to load automatically:
  - Monitor p95 latency
  - If > 100ms, add workers
  - If idle, remove workers
  - Min 5, max 50 workers
TestCases:
  - Heavy load -> p95 high -> add workers
  - Light load -> p95 low -> remove workers
  - Stay within bounds
Tags: adaptive, auto-scaling

// SECTION 19.2.16-19.2.30: Shared State & Thread-Safety

[19.2.16]
Problem: Shared Counter with Arc<Mutex<>>
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Multiple actors increment shared counter:
  - 3 actors, each increment 100 times
  - Expected result = 300
  - Verify no data races
TestCases:
  - 3 threads, 100 increments each -> result = 300
  - Enable ThreadSanitizer -> no data races
Tags: arc-mutex, shared-state

[19.2.17]
Problem: Shared Cache with Arc<RwLock<>>
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Multiple readers, few writers:
  - 10 reader threads
  - 1 writer thread
  - Readers don't block each other
  - Measure lock contention
TestCases:
  - 10 readers concurrent -> fast
  - Writer waits for readers -> no writer starve
Tags: rwlock, cache

[19.2.18]
Problem: Shared Metrics Collector
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  All actors update shared metrics:
  - Latency histogram (100 buckets)
  - Lock contention minimal
  - Query metrics without stopping
TestCases:
  - 50 actors updating metrics
  - Query histogram concurrently
  - No blocking
Tags: metrics, thread-safe

[19.2.19]
Problem: Shared Configuration
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  All actors share config:
  - Config can be updated
  - Actors see changes immediately
  - Hot-reload without restart
TestCases:
  - Update config -> all actors see it
  - Verify consistency
Tags: shared-config, hot-reload

[19.2.20]
Problem: Shared State Synchronization
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Actor A changes state -> Actor B reads:
  - A writes value
  - B reads same value
  - Verify consistency
  - Use proper memory ordering
TestCases:
  - A: set(x, 42)
  - B: get(x) == 42
  - Guarantee even under races
Tags: synchronization, consistency

[19.2.21]
Problem: Atomic Shutdown Flag
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Signal shutdown to all actors:
  - One flag, many readers
  - Set once, read N times
  - No lock needed
TestCases:
  - Set shutdown flag -> all actors see it
  - Measure latency to see shutdown
Tags: atomic, flag

[19.2.22]
Problem: Barrier Synchronization
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  3 threads wait for each other:
  - All reach barrier
  - Barrier releases all together
  - Measure overhead
TestCases:
  - 3 threads at barrier -> all wait
  - Last arrival unblocks all
  - Overhead < 1µs
Tags: barrier, synchronization

[19.2.23]
Problem: Rendezvous Point
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Two threads meet at point:
  - Thread A reaches first, waits
  - Thread B arrives, both continue
  - Both proceed together
TestCases:
  - A reaches at t=100ms, B at t=200ms
  - Both continue at t=200ms
Tags: synchronization, checkpoint

[19.2.24]
Problem: Fairness Monitor
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Track lock access fairness:
  - Which actors get locks?
  - Detect starvation
  - Ensure fair scheduling
TestCases:
  - 50 actors contending lock
  - Each should get ~2% of lock time
  - Variance < 10%
Tags: fairness, scheduling

[19.2.25]
Problem: Adaptive Lock Strategy
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Spin-lock vs. sleep based on contention:
  - Spin: busy-wait (fast for short holds)
  - Sleep: OS sleep (efficient for long waits)
  - Switch based on measured contention
TestCases:
  - Low contention -> spin lock
  - High contention -> sleep lock
  - P99 latency similar both cases
Tags: optimization, lock-choice

[19.2.26]
Problem: Lock-Free Counter
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Use atomic without Mutex:
  - Multiple threads increment
  - No lock waits
  - Measure performance vs. Mutex
TestCases:
  - Compare: Atomic vs. Mutex<usize>
  - Atomic ~10x faster
Tags: lock-free, atomic

[19.2.27]
Problem: Seqlock (Sequence Lock)
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  Readers don't block writers:
  - Reader checks sequence before/after
  - If changed, retry
  - Writer: increment seq, write, increment seq
TestCases:
  - Heavy readers, light writer
  - Readers never block
  - P99 reader latency  < 1µs
Tags: seqlock, lock-free

[19.2.28]
Problem: Read-Write Lock Patterns
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Multiple readers, single writer:
  - 10 readers, 1 writer
  - Readers don't block each other
  - Writer exclusive
  - Benchmark vs. Mutex
TestCases:
  - 10 readers concurrent -> faster than Mutex
Tags: rwlock, read-heavy

[19.2.29]
Problem: Transactional Update (Optimistic)
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Read-modify-write without holding lock:
  - Read state
  - Modify locally
  - Commit if unchanged
  - Retry if changed
TestCases:
  - High contention -> many retries
  - Low contention -> few retries
Tags: optimistic-concurrency, transaction

[19.2.30]
Problem: Consensus Protocol
Difficulty: 3/5
Description:
  N actors vote on decision:
  - Majority wins
  - Measure convergence time
  - Handle ties
TestCases:
  - 5 actors, 3 vote "yes" -> consensus = yes
  - 2.5 actors vote "no" -> consensus = no (ties)
Tags: consensus, voting

===== CATEGORY 19.3: SYNCHRONIZATION (20 Problems) =====

// SECTION 19.3.1-19.3.10: Arc & Mutex Patterns (Continued from 19.2.16-19.2.20, now advanced)

[19.3.1]
Problem: Arc Clone Refcount Tracking
Difficulty: 1/5
Description:
  Create Arc, clone 5 times:
  - Initial refcount = 1
  - After 5 clones = 6
  - Drop one -> refcount = 5
  - All dropped -> deallocate
TestCases:
  - Create Arc<i32>(42)
  - Clone 5 times
  - Check refcount == 6
Tags: arc-basics, refcount

[19.3.2]
Problem: Arc<Mutex<>> Shared Data
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Share mutable data across threads:
  - Create Arc<Mutex<i32>>
  - Clone to 3 threads
  - Each increments 100x
  - Result = 300
TestCases:
  - 3 threads, 100 increments each
  - Final value = 300
Tags: arc-mutex, thread-safe

[19.3.3]
Problem: Arc<RwLock<>> Cache
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Cache with multiple readers:
  - 10 reader threads
  - 1 writer thread
  - Readers don't block each other
  - Measure throughput
TestCases:
  - 10 readers: 1000 reads/sec each
  - 1 writer: 10 writes/sec
  - Total: > 10,000 reads/sec
Tags: rwlock, cache

[19.3.4]
Problem: Arc<AtomicUsize> Lock-Free
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Non-blocking counter:
  - 4 threads increment shared atomic
  - No locks held
  - Measure latency vs. Mutex
TestCases:
  - Atomic: < 100ns per increment
  - Mutex: ~500ns per increment
  - Atomic 5x faster
Tags: atomic, lock-free

[19.3.5]
Problem: Arc Object Pool
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Reuse Arc instances:
  - Create 100 Arcs
  - Distribute and collect
  - Measure allocation savings
TestCases:
  - Pool reduces alloc by 80%
Tags: pooling, reuse

[19.3.6]
Problem: Nested Arc<Mutex<>>
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Arc<Mutex<Vec<Arc<Mutex<i32>>>>>:
  - Inner Arc<Mutex<>> allows independent locks
  - Deadlock risk if not careful
  - Lock in strict order
TestCases:
  - Create nested structure
  - Access inner elements
  - No deadlock
Tags: nesting, inner-mutability

[19.3.7]
Problem: Deadlock Scenario Detection
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Demonstrate deadlock:
  - Thread A: lock L1 then L2
  - Thread B: lock L2 then L1
  - Program hangs
  - (For demo: catch hang or explain it)
TestCases:
  - Code structure shows deadlock potential
  - Timeout detects hang
Tags: deadlock-demo

[19.3.8]
Problem: Arc with Custom Drop
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Track when Arc dropped:
  - Custom struct with Drop impl
  - When refcount = 0, run cleanup
  - Useful for resources
TestCases:
  - Arc<File> ->  file closes on final drop
  - Track close events
Tags: drop-impl, resource-cleanup

[19.3.9]
Problem: Weak References
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Prevent circular references:
  - Parent has Arc<Child>
  - Child has Weak<Parent>
  - Child can upgrade() to Arc if parent alive
  - Prevents HANG on cycle
TestCases:
  - Create parent-child
  - Child upgrades to parent
  - Parent drops -> children isolated
Tags: weak-ref, cycles

[19.3.10]
Problem: Arc Wrapper Pattern
Difficulty: 2/5
Description:
  Encapsulate sync details:
  - Hide Arc<Mutex<T>> behind API
  - Safe, simple interface
  - Threads clone wrapper, use safely
TestCases:
  - Users don't see Mutex
  - Automatic synchronization
Tags: encapsulation, pattern

